WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.816
We are at a turning point in transportation.&nbsp;
Although gas cars still dominate the roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.816 --> 00:00:12.696
it's becoming increasingly clear that the&nbsp;
future of transport is electric and a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.696 --> 00:00:19.386
this excitement is because of one company. Tesla&nbsp;
is disrupting the automotive industry by creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:28.266
affordable EVs of all kinds. But it almost didn't&nbsp;
make it. Success was a longshot and Elon Musk knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.266 --> 00:00:35.136
it. When we first started Tesla and SolarCity we&nbsp;
thought they would fail. Tesla, in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.136 --> 00:00:41.586
we thought probably we've maybe 10% chance of&nbsp;
success. This is not the story of a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.586 --> 00:00:48.576
that nearly died many times. This is the story of&nbsp;
a man who would not give up. This is the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.576 --> 00:00:56.406
Elon Musk's perseverance against all odds. In&nbsp;
2003, in Silicon Valley, two engineers created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.406 --> 00:01:02.286
Tesla. Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning. They&nbsp;
named it after Serbian-American inventor Nikola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.286 --> 00:01:08.766
Tesla. Back then, Tesla was called Tesla Motors.&nbsp;
They saw an opportunity to create an electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.766 --> 00:01:14.106
car after General Motors recalled the EV1 - the&nbsp;
first mass-produced electric car in the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:19.476
era. It's unclear why GM did this - it could have&nbsp;
been because of a lack of demand, high production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:25.356
costs, or pressure from the oil and gas industry.&nbsp;
Either way, GM crushed the cars in a junkyard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:30.000
Elon came into the picture when the founders went&nbsp;
looking for funding. He made millions when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.450
financial services company he co-founded, x.com,&nbsp;
merged with a rival and became PayPal. When eBay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:44.070
bought PayPal in 2002, Elon walked away with $180&nbsp;
million. He was very successful. But instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:51.360
calling it a career, he sunk all his winnings into&nbsp;
new ventures. He put $100 million into SpaceX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.710
$10 million into SolarCity, and $70 million&nbsp;
into Tesla including $6.5 million at the start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:02.600
and became the chairman of the company's board.&nbsp;
So, all of the money just came from me personally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:08.180
I didn't want to ask people - other investors&nbsp;
- for money if I thought we were going to die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:14.530
2006 was a big year for Tesla. The roadster&nbsp;
made its official appearance: a good-looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:21.130
fast electric car. But there were quality issues&nbsp;
and a lot of production problems. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.140
Eberhard was ousted as CEO before the Roadster&nbsp;
even went into production which didn't happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:33.190
until 2008. Tesla was running out of money. The&nbsp;
company budgeted $25 million for the development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:39.700
of the Roadster but it ended up costing nearly&nbsp;
five times that. By the fall of 2008, Tesla had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:46.420
only $9 million left to fund the entire company.&nbsp;
It was a tumultuous time with multiple CEOs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:52.720
coming and going and in the end, Elon was left in&nbsp;
charge. He said this in an interview about the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:59.110
decision he had to make: "...do I let Tesla die or&nbsp;
do I make it live with a lot of pain and grief?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:06.520
He chose to make Tesla live but it couldn't have&nbsp;
been more painful. One of America's largest banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.620
Lehman Brothers, collapsed, sinking the world&nbsp;
economy into the worst recession since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.590
Great Depression. Plus, SpaceX was close to being&nbsp;
bankrupt after three failed rocket launches. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:23.830
he was going through a divorce from his wife&nbsp;
Justine. Elon said 2008 was the worst year of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:28.990
his life and he never thought he was someone&nbsp;
capable of having a nervous breakdown but came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:35.410
close to it. Elon fought hard to save his&nbsp;
companies. He cut 25% of Tesla's workforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:41.860
and asked friends to help cover payroll. But by&nbsp;
December 2008, Tesla was about to die. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:47.650
he didn't give up. He scraped together $20 million&nbsp;
from various sources, including the money he made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.300
investing in his cousin's data center startup&nbsp;
Everdream which Dell had bought the year before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:59.020
Investors agreed to match what he cobbled up and&nbsp;
in the end, Elon managed to secure a $40 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:04.390
round of financing on Christmas Eve. It was the&nbsp;
last hour of the last day that it was possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:10.090
Ashlee Vance wrote a biography about Elon Musk&nbsp;
and there's one dinner conversation that Elon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:15.410
had with a Tesla investor that really shows&nbsp;
his tenacity. During that dinner, Elon said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:21.740
I will spend my last dollar on these companies. If&nbsp;
we have to move into Justine's parents' basement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:29.090
we'll do it. Tesla barely pulled through in 2008&nbsp;
but needed more money to stay alive. That came in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:36.230
May 2009 when German automaker Daimler invested&nbsp;
$15 million by taking a 10% stake in the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:41.870
Daimler and Tesla had already been working on an&nbsp;
electric smart car. The company also received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:47.960
$465 million loan from the U.S. Department of&nbsp;
Energy which had a program backing clean tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:54.200
startups. The following year, Tesla found a more&nbsp;
stable solution when it went public at $17 a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:00.050
share. Despite the problems with the Roadster,&nbsp;
Tesla had achieved something no company had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.134 --> 00:05:06.074
ever done before.&nbsp;It proved that electric cars&nbsp;
could be cool and fast. But the Roadster was too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.074 --> 00:05:12.404
expensive for most people - costing more than&nbsp;
$100,000. So, they built another car. In 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.404 --> 00:05:18.884
Tesla stunned the world when it began shipping&nbsp;
the Model S sedan. It took everyone's breath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:24.704
away. The Model S could go more than 300 miles on&nbsp;
a single charge and it was more affordable than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.704 --> 00:05:29.594
the Roadster although still expensive. Tesla's&nbsp;
problems were still not over and nearly went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:35.204
bankrupt again in 2013. Even though the Model&nbsp;
S was in high demand, customers who put down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.204 --> 00:05:40.634
$5,000 deposit were hesitant about going through&nbsp;
with their purchase. There were a lot of reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.634 --> 00:05:45.644
for this: complaints about the interior, kinks&nbsp;
with the car, some of the earliest versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:50.564
didn't have parking sensors and cruise control&nbsp;
which are common in high-end vehicles. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.564 --> 00:05:55.334
also concerns about the resale value. We were&nbsp;
operating with maybe one to two weeks of money.

00:05:58.451 --> 00:06:04.301
When Elon found out about the problem he did&nbsp;
three things. First, he turned every Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.301 --> 00:06:09.101
employee into a salesperson. They called&nbsp;
everyone who put down a deposit and tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:13.548
to convince them to go through with buying the&nbsp;
car. Second, he convinced his friend Larry Page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.548 --> 00:06:19.368
the CEO of Google, to buy Tesla if the company&nbsp;
actually did run out of money. Finally, Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.368 --> 00:06:24.708
shut down its factory when orders weren't coming&nbsp;
in fast enough but did not admit this publicly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:28.800
or else it would have scared investors and&nbsp;
sent the stock price plummeting. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.510
Tesla said it was closing down for maintenance&nbsp;
which was technically true but not the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:41.010
story. The strategy worked. On May 8, 2013, Tesla&nbsp;
stunned Wall Street by posting its first-ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:48.900
profit as a public company. Tesla made $11 million&nbsp;
on $562 million in sales after delivering 4,900&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:56.310
Model S's in the first quarter. Elon saved Tesla&nbsp;
from death again. But there were more hardships to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:02.160
come. The company's problems were still not over&nbsp;
and nearly went bankrupt again in 2018. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.410
it had to do with the Model 3. Elon's vision from&nbsp;
the beginning was to build an affordable electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:13.260
car paid for by the more expensive cars - the&nbsp;
Model S and also the Model X which came out a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:21.450
few years earlier. The Model 3 had a base price of&nbsp;
$35,000. This is kind of crazy but I just learnt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:26.730
I was just told that the total number&nbsp;
of orders for the Model 3 in the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:33.090
24 hours has now passed 115,000. But the&nbsp;
huge demand also meant the company had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:40.980
produce the cars in time. And frankly, we're&nbsp;
gonna be in production hell. Welcome, welcome.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.280
As the saying goes, if you're&nbsp;
going through hell, keep going.

00:07:46.642 --> 00:07:52.823
Tesla struggled to meet its production targets. It&nbsp;
initially wanted to produce 5,000 Model 3s a week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.823 --> 00:08:00.134
but in the first three months of 2018, it made&nbsp;
just 9,800 of them. Tesla was bleeding money as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.134 --> 00:08:05.684
tried to increase production of the car. It lost&nbsp;
$1 billion dollars in the first half of 2019 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.684 --> 00:08:12.164
scrambled to raise capital. Elon said the company&nbsp;
was close to death within single-digit weeks. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.164 --> 00:08:17.564
even slept on the factory floor because he didn't&nbsp;
have time to go home and also because he says he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.564 --> 00:08:23.054
wanted to suffer more than any other employee.&nbsp;
Despite the torturous production process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.054 --> 00:08:29.114
Tesla managed to pull through once again. It&nbsp;
turned a corner in the second half of 2019 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.114 --> 00:08:34.934
delivering over 92,000 Model 3s in the fourth&nbsp;
quarter - that's an increase from the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:41.984
period the year before or a year-over-year of&nbsp;
46%. And the latest model - the Model Y - was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.984 --> 00:08:48.674
ahead of schedule. Tesla has managed to survive.&nbsp;
Now it's looking ahead to many ambitious plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.674 --> 00:08:55.184
including fully autonomous driving, a million-mile&nbsp;
battery, and driverless cabs called robotaxis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.334 --> 00:09:01.334
Tesla's stock price hovers around $1,000 a share&nbsp;
as of the making of this video but the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.334 --> 00:09:07.784
bulls see it going up way more. Tesla has endured&nbsp;
many near-death experiences and through it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.784 --> 00:09:12.734
Elon has never ever given up. It's&nbsp;
hard to stay alive as a car company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.734 --> 00:09:21.404
honestly. Not only is Tesla alive but because&nbsp;
of his vision, ambition, and relentless drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.404 --> 00:09:26.984
its future is still being written. Thanks so&nbsp;much&nbsp;
for watching, I'm Cindy Pom. If you liked what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.984 --> 00:09:30.704
you saw, give us a like, and don't forget to&nbsp;
subscribe to my new channel. While you're at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.704 --> 00:09:33.764
hit that notification bell so you'll&nbsp;
know when I upload a new video. I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.764 --> 00:09:37.094
to do a lot more stories on topics that&nbsp;
I find super interesting. See you soon.

